\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Example with $\theta =2$. Same-colored items are within $\epsilon $ of each other, while differently colored items are not. For $\mathcal  {L}=\{\ell _1,\ell _2,\ell _3\}$, $N(\mathcal  {L})=\{r_1,r_3,r_4,r_7\}$ contains only red items, so $M_{\mathcal  {L}}<\epsilon $. Every user has at least $\theta $ neighbors, but the users do not share a common neighborhood: no biclique larger than $2\times 1$ exists on these users. Of the 12 possible edges between $\mathcal  {L}$ and $N(\mathcal  {L})$, only 6 exist. Thus, the example illustrates how the model can capture users connected to similar, but not identical, items.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:epsilon-cohesive-core}{{3}{5}{}{figure.3}{}}
